userStories.description:
**Goal** — Define the permission declaration contract, its pure violation checker and the exact five temporary pending-surface exceptions.

**Motivation** — The contract and matrix tests are its safety net.

**Approach** — `apps/api/src/permissions/catalog.ts` gains:

```ts
/**
 * Role keys whose surface arrives in a later phase: approvals and their policies (P3), the audit log (P2),
 * and deciding an assistant action as an approval type (P3). No route, tool or system writer declares
 * them yet; the catalog contract test refuses an undeclared key unless it is listed here, and refuses a
 * listed key once a surface declares it, so the list shrinks as P2 and P3 land.
 */
export const PENDING_SURFACE_KEYS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([
  'approval.decide',
  'approval.policy.read',
  'approval.policy.manage',
  'audit.read',
  'fleet.approval.decide.assistant',
]);
```

New file `apps/api/src/permissions/test-helpers/permission-contract.ts` (test support, next to `in-memory-permissions.ts`). US-006 creates it with the types and `findContractViolations`; US-007 adds `collectPermissionDeclarations`:

```ts
export interface RouteDeclaration {
  /** `"<METHOD> /<controller path>/<handler path>"`, for violation messages. */
  route: string;
  /** Handler metadata, else class metadata, as RequiresGuard reads it. */
  requires: RequiresMetadata | undefined;
  /** Whether RequiresGuard is among the class's or the handler's `@UseGuards`. */
  hasRequiresGuard: boolean;
}
export interface ToolDeclaration { tool: string; key: string }
export interface SystemWriterDeclaration { writer: string; key: string }
export interface PermissionDeclarations {
  routes: readonly RouteDeclaration[];
  tools: readonly ToolDeclaration[];
  systemWriters: readonly SystemWriterDeclaration[];
}

export type ContractViolation =
  | { kind: 'route-without-key'; route: string }
  | { kind: 'unknown-key'; source: string; key: string }
  | { kind: 'guard-missing'; route: string }
  | { kind: 'unused-key'; key: string }
  | { kind: 'pending-key-declared'; source: string; key: string };

/** Walks a booted Nest app's controllers and providers, and the tool registry. */
export function collectPermissionDeclarations(
  modules: ModulesContainer,
  registry: AssistantToolRegistry,
): PermissionDeclarations;

/** Every violation; callers compare as a set (the order is not part of the contract). */
export function findContractViolations(
  declarations: PermissionDeclarations,
  catalog: readonly PermissionCatalogEntry[] = PERMISSION_CATALOG,
  pending: ReadonlySet<string> = PENDING_SURFACE_KEYS,
): ContractViolation[];
```

The collector signature above is the shared interface delivered in US-007; this story implements the types and checker only, not an empty collector stub.

**`findContractViolations` rules:**
1. A route whose `requires` is undefined is `route-without-key`.
2. Every key of a route, tool or writer that is not the `key` of an entry in the `catalog` argument is `unknown-key`, with `source` the route, tool name or writer class name. (The checker never calls the global `findCatalogEntry`, so a unit test can pass a small catalog of made-up keys.)
3. A route whose `requires.enforcedBy` is `'guard'` and whose `hasRequiresGuard` is false is `guard-missing`.
4. Every catalog key declared by no route, tool or writer, and not in `pending`, is `unused-key`.
5. Every key in `pending` that some route, tool or writer declares is `pending-key-declared`.

Add permission-contract.spec.ts beside this source helper. Synthetic fixtures use catalog entries k.one and k.two, pending empty unless stated, with guard-enforced/hasRequiresGuard=true routes unless the case changes it. Compare violations as a set: enumeration order is not public behavior. A missing requires declaration does not license bypassing unused catalog checks. A service declaration can omit RequiresGuard; unknown keys and pending declarations must still be reported for tools and writers as well as routes. The pending set is not a permanent allowlist: remove an exception once a later surface declares it, enforced by pending-key-declared. US-001 supplies the post-fold catalog before this story runs.

**Scope** — In: PENDING_SURFACE_KEYS, declaration and violation types, pure findContractViolations and synthetic unit behavior. Out: runtime collection, registry.list and booted-app contract (US-007), route annotations (US-002/003), SystemWriter decorator (US-004) and matrix (US-005).
- Surfaces for the five pending keys (approval decide and policies, the audit log, assistant-action approvals) are out of scope (P2, P3).

**Interface** — The exact TypeScript declarations and checker defaults above are the seam consumed by US-007; collectPermissionDeclarations is explicitly implemented only in US-007.

**Goal** — Collect real Nest route/provider/tool declarations and assert the booted API has no permission catalog contract violations.

**Motivation** — The contract and matrix tests are its safety net.

**Approach** — Extend `apps/api/src/permissions/test-helpers/permission-contract.ts`, created by US-006, with the runtime collector. Shared interface from the design:

```ts
export interface RouteDeclaration {
  /** `"<METHOD> /<controller path>/<handler path>"`, for violation messages. */
  route: string;
  /** Handler metadata, else class metadata, as RequiresGuard reads it. */
  requires: RequiresMetadata | undefined;
  /** Whether RequiresGuard is among the class's or the handler's `@UseGuards`. */
  hasRequiresGuard: boolean;
}
export interface ToolDeclaration { tool: string; key: string }
export interface SystemWriterDeclaration { writer: string; key: string }
export interface PermissionDeclarations {
  routes: readonly RouteDeclaration[];
  tools: readonly ToolDeclaration[];
  systemWriters: readonly SystemWriterDeclaration[];
}

export type ContractViolation =
  | { kind: 'route-without-key'; route: string }
  | { kind: 'unknown-key'; source: string; key: string }
  | { kind: 'guard-missing'; route: string }
  | { kind: 'unused-key'; key: string }
  | { kind: 'pending-key-declared'; source: string; key: string };

/** Walks a booted Nest app's controllers and providers, and the tool registry. */
export function collectPermissionDeclarations(
  modules: ModulesContainer,
  registry: AssistantToolRegistry,
): PermissionDeclarations;

/** Every violation; callers compare as a set (the order is not part of the contract). */
export function findContractViolations(
  declarations: PermissionDeclarations,
  catalog: readonly PermissionCatalogEntry[] = PERMISSION_CATALOG,
  pending: ReadonlySet<string> = PENDING_SURFACE_KEYS,
): ContractViolation[];
```

**`collectPermissionDeclarations`:**
- For every module in `ModulesContainer`, each controller wrapper's `metatype` contributes one `RouteDeclaration` per prototype method carrying Nest's `METHOD_METADATA`. The path comes from `PATH_METADATA` on the class and the method, and the method name from `RequestMethod`.
- Each provider wrapper contributes a `SystemWriterDeclaration` when `SYSTEM_WRITER_METADATA` is set on its `metatype` or on `instance.constructor`. The second source covers factory-built providers such as the FTS optimize strategy.
- A class reached through several wrappers (`useExisting` aliases, re-exports) is reported once.
- Tools come from `registry.list()`, as `{ tool: descriptor.name, key }`.

`AssistantToolRegistry` (`apps/api/src/fleet/assistant/assistant-tool.registry.ts`) gains `list(): readonly AssistantTool[]`, which returns the registered tools in registration order.

Use Nest's ModulesContainer from @nestjs/core; PATH_METADATA, METHOD_METADATA and GUARDS_METADATA from @nestjs/common/constants; RequestMethod from @nestjs/common. Resolve handler Requires metadata before class metadata, exactly as RequiresGuard does. hasRequiresGuard is true if RequiresGuard appears in either class or handler guards. Construct readable '<METHOD> /<controller path>/<handler path>' route names without duplicated path separators. Deduplicate by class identity across wrappers, not by key, since distinct classes may legitimately declare the same system key. Factory providers require inspecting the initialized instance constructor; do not filter them out because their wrapper metatype is a factory. Keep excluded-from-OpenAPI and SSE controller routes in the walk. Do not use Swagger as the discovery source or enumerate a manually maintained route whitelist.

US-006's pure checker remains the consumer of these declarations: missing route keys, unknown keys, missing guards for guard enforcement, unused catalog keys except pending, and declared pending keys are violations. The default pending set contains only approval.decide, approval.policy.read, approval.policy.manage, audit.read and fleet.approval.decide.assistant. No extra exemptions. The declaration work of US-002/003/004 is required before the full-app assertion, and US-006 transitively brings US-001's fold.

Extend assistant-tool.registry.spec.ts for empty and ordered list outcomes while preserving get results and the existing Error('koda tool already registered: <name>') duplicate-name refusal. Add permission-contract.collector.spec.ts beside the helper with initialized miniature Nest testing modules for provider metadata, handler-over-class fallback, tool extraction and useExisting deduplication; these are DB-free unit tests, not integration tests. Add `apps/api/test/integration/permissions/permission-contract.integration.spec.ts`: reset DB before bootHttpApp, retrieve app.get(ModulesContainer) and app.get(AssistantToolRegistry), collect after initialization and assert no violations. Also pin runner.sync, thread.read, role.binding.owner and system.schedule.dispatch, plus the default CounterOptimizeStrategy factory writer. Use default FTS_OPTIMIZE_STRATEGY (counter) in that assertion, restoring any test environment changes. Close the app after the suite. Boot seeds system roles; run this DB-backed spec with test:scoped and the compose test Postgres available. Do not weaken the contract if it exposes a missed declaration.

**Scope** — In: additive registry.list, metadata-based collector, synthetic collector unit behavior and the booted-app contract integration. Out: rewriting checker rules/pending exceptions (US-006), implementing route/writer annotations instead of consuming them (US-002/003/004), matrix (US-005), key folding (US-001), production authorization changes, audit and approval surfaces or legacy deletion.

**Interface** — Implement `collectPermissionDeclarations(modules: ModulesContainer, registry: AssistantToolRegistry): PermissionDeclarations` above and `list(): readonly AssistantTool[]` on AssistantToolRegistry; consume US-006's `findContractViolations` and US-004's `SYSTEM_WRITER_METADATA` without alternative metadata channels.
